        "id": "ch8_4_3-1",
        "text": "Making parameters mandatory\nWe might want our script to require an explicit value for the \u2013City parameter each time we run it.\nWe could do that by changing the -City parameter like this:\n[CmdletBinding()]\nParam(\n[Parameter(Mandatory = $true)]\n$City = \"London\"\n)\nWe\u2019ve added the line [Parameter(Mandatory = $true)] before the $City parameter. Note\nthat there is no comma ( , ) after the attribute. Now we\u2019ve done that, the script ignores the default value\nfor the parameter ( \"London\" ), and it prompts for a city:\nWorking with parameters\n205\nFigure 8.4 \u2013 Mandatory parameters\nIn the figure, we can see that because we didn\u2019t include a value for the -City parameter, we get\nprompted. One of the most useful things we can do with a script is to make it accept parameter values\nfrom the pipeline. Let\u2019s have a look at how we can do that.",
